<!-- 3. AEROSOL ENGINEERING: CANS, VALVES, AND ACTUATORS SELECTION -->
<section class="section" id="3-aerosol-engineering-cans-valves-and-actuators-selection">
  <h2>3. Aerosol Engineering: Cans, Valves, and Actuators Selection</h2>
  <p>Selecting the packaging hardware is critical to ensure can integrity and a controlled spray pattern. High-SPF formulas often require specialized Bag-on-Valve (BoV) systems, which separate the product from the propellant gas. This prevents gas oxidation of active ingredients and allows 360-degree spray convenience. The core packaging components include:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Can material</strong> — aluminium resists corrosion in humid environments; tinplate is more cost-effective but oxidation-prone</li>
    <li><strong>Valve type</strong> — standard continuous valves mix concentrate with propellant, while Bag-on-Valve isolates the two</li>
    <li><strong>Internal coatings</strong> — required to protect the metal from the sunscreen's active organic compounds</li>
    <li><strong>Actuator design</strong> — swirl-chamber geometry sets droplet size and spray pattern</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The selection of the physical packaging components is just as critical to the product's performance as the chemical formulation. An aerosol container acts as a pressurized packaging system where the can, valve assembly, and actuator nozzle work together to release the formulation. Selecting incompatible components can result in slow chemical <a href="/aerosol-can-corrosion-1/">corrosion</a>, propellant leaks, mechanical valve failures, or poor spray atomization. Product founders must evaluate these engineering components to guarantee structural safety, chemical stability, and a consistent user experience throughout the product's shelf life.</p>

            <h3>Packaging Materials: Aluminum vs. Tinplate Cans for Sunscreen</h3>
            <p>Aluminum cans are the industry gold standard for cosmetic and personal care products due to their sleek, seamless appearance and excellent resistance to rusting in humid bathrooms or beach environments. Tinplate cans, constructed from sheets of steel coated with thin layers of tin, are more cost-effective but are prone to external oxidation. For a full breakdown of how the two metals compare on cost, pressure performance, and corrosion resistance, see our <a href="/aluminium-vs-tinplate-aerosol-cans/">aluminium vs tinplate aerosol cans</a> guide. Regardless of the metal selected, sunscreens contain active organic compounds that require advanced internal coatings to prevent corrosion and protect the formulation.</p>

            <h3>Valve Mechanics: Continuous Spray, Bag-on-Valve (BoV), and 360-Degree Delivery</h3>
            <p>Standard continuous aerosol valves mix the sunscreen concentrate directly with the propellant gas inside the can, spraying them out together. In contrast, <a href="/bag-on-valve-technology-design-and-applications/">Bag-on-Valve (BoV) technology</a> isolates the sunscreen concentrate inside a multi-layered plastic/aluminum bag welded directly to the valve, using compressed air or nitrogen in the surrounding space to squeeze the bag. BoV systems provide 100% product evacuation, allow continuous spraying at any angle (even upside down), and prevent the propellant from mixing with the skin formula.</p>

            <h3>Actuator Selection for Optimized Particle Size Distribution and Fine Mist</h3>
            <p>The actuator is the plastic nozzle clicked onto the valve stem that controls the shape, speed, and droplet size of the spray. It contains a miniature swirl chamber that forces the liquid to spin rapidly, causing it to break apart into small, uniform droplets as it exits the orifice. For sunscreen sprays, the actuator must be selected to balance fine atomization with inhalation safety, producing droplets large enough to settle on the skin rather than floating in the air as inhalable mist.</p>
        </section>



<!-- 4. PROPELLANT SELECTION AND GAS DYNAMICS -->
<section class="section" id="4-propellant-selection-and-gas-dynamics">
  <h2>4. Propellant Selection and Gas Dynamics</h2>
  <p>Propellants dictate the pressure decay, atomization quality, and skin feel of the sunscreen spray. Bag-on-Valve systems typically use compressed Nitrogen or air, while traditional aerosols utilize <a href="/aerosol-propellants-explained-lpg-vs-dme-vs-hfc-vs-n2-vs-co2/">liquefied hydrocarbon gases</a>. Balancing the internal pressure prevents droplet inhalation hazards at the nozzle. Key propellant parameters include:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>LPG blends</strong> — cost-effective, chemically stable, and insoluble in water</li>
    <li><strong>DME</strong> — a dual propellant/solvent role but flammable and reliant on explosion-proof filling lines</li>
    <li><strong>Compressed gases</strong> — nitrogen and CO₂ are non-flammable and common in Bag-on-Valve designs</li>
    <li><strong>Regulatory limits</strong> — VOC content is capped by regional consumer-product regulations</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Propellents provide the thermodynamic pressure required to push the active formulation out of the container and break it into a spray. Founders can choose between liquefied petroleum gases, which maintain a steady pressure during use, or eco-friendly compressed gases, which exhibit a gradual drop in pressure as the can empties. The choice of propellant impacts the flammability rating, formulation solubility, chemical stability, and environmental compliance of the finished consumer product.</p>

            <h3>Liquefied Petroleum Gas (LPG) vs. Dimethyl Ether (DME) in Sunscreens</h3>
            <p>Liquefied petroleum gas (LPG) blends are highly popular because they are cost-effective, chemically stable, and insoluble in water, which helps preserve the water-resistant properties of the sunscreen film. Dimethyl ether (DME) is a highly polar liquefied gas that exhibits excellent solvency, allowing it to dissolve both water and organic UV filters directly. However, DME is highly flammable, requires specialized explosion-proof manufacturing lines governed by workplace <a href="https://www.osha.gov/chemical-hazards" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">chemical hazard controls</a>, and can attack certain rubber gaskets and internal can coatings.</p>

            <h3>Compressed Gases (Nitrogen, Carbon Dioxide) and Bag-on-Valve Applications</h3>
            <p>Compressed gases like Nitrogen (N2) and Carbon Dioxide (CO2) are non-flammable, eco-friendly alternatives that are increasingly utilized in modern personal care formulations. In Bag-on-Valve (BoV) packages, compressed nitrogen occupies the space between the bag and the inner can wall, exerting constant pressure without mixing with the sunscreen. Because these gases do not liquefy under pressure, they do not contribute to Volatile Organic Compound (VOC) limits, simplifying global regulatory compliance.</p>

            <h3>Safety, Flammability, and VOC Regulatory Limits</h3>
            <p>Sunscreens formulated with high-solvency alcohols and liquefied propellants are classified as highly flammable aerosols, demanding strict manufacturing safeguards and warning labels. Regulatory bodies limit the total percentage of Volatile Organic Compounds (VOCs) permitted in consumer personal care products to control air quality. Brand founders must work closely with experienced packaging engineers to formulate products that meet these regional VOC limits without sacrificing atomization performance.</p>

            
        </section>



<!-- 5. THE MANUFACTURING AND FILLING PROCESS -->
<section class="section" id="5-the-manufacturing-and-filling-process">
  <h2>5. The Manufacturing and Filling Process</h2>
  <p>Manufacturing aerosol sunscreens requires specialized lines, particularly when filling Bag-on-Valve systems. The valve is crimped onto the can and pressurized with gas before the concentrate is filled inside the bag. This unique sequence prevents product contamination and ensures accurate pressure levels. Key steps in the manufacturing sequence include:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Compounding</strong> — temperature-controlled blending under GMP with batch QC release testing</li>
    <li><strong>Filling method</strong> — pressure filling or under-cup filling, chosen for solvent and production-speed needs</li>
    <li><strong>Bag-on-Valve sequence</strong> — bag-and-valve insertion, gas pressurization, crimping, then concentrate filling</li>
    <li><strong>Quality checks</strong> — specific gravity, viscosity, and active concentration verified before release</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Aerosol filling is a highly specialized manufacturing process that requires dedicated, explosion-proof production lines and strict safety protocols to handle pressurized gases — most founders access this capacity through a specialist partner rather than building it in-house, as explained in our guide to <a href="/how-contract-aerosol-filling-works/">how contract aerosol filling works</a>. Compounding the liquid concentrate must follow strict Good Manufacturing Practices (GMP) to ensure chemical uniformity, batch-to-batch consistency, and microbial purity. Once the concentrate is blended, the cans are filled and pressurized using specialized volumetric machinery, followed by thorough quality control checks to identify potential leaks.</p>

            <h3>Compounding and Quality Control of the Concentrate</h3>
            <p>Compounding begins in stainless steel mixing vessels where UV filters, solvents, emollients, and active ingredients are blended into a uniform solution. This process requires precise temperature controls, as some chemical filters must be gently heated to melt and dissolve completely without thermal degradation. Before proceeding to the filling line, the batch must pass laboratory QC tests verifying correct specific gravity, viscosity, refractive index, and active filter concentrations.</p>

            <h3>Filling Technologies: Cold Filling vs. Pressure Filling vs. Under-Cup Filling</h3>
            <p>Cold filling cools both the active concentrate and liquefied propellants to extreme sub-zero temperatures (often below -30°C) before pouring them into the can, followed by crimping the valve; however, this is rarely used for sunscreens due to solvent crystallization risks. Pressure filling inserts the liquid concentrate first, crimps the valve, and then forces the propellant through the valve stem at high pressure. Under-Cup filling raises the valve cup slightly, injects the propellant under vacuum, and instantly crimps the valve in place, offering high production speeds.</p>

            <h3>Bag-on-Valve (BoV) Filling Walkthrough</h3>
            <p>For Bag-on-Valve (BoV) products, the filling process is structurally different. First, the empty bag-and-valve assembly is inserted into the can, and pressurized air or nitrogen is injected into the container around the bag. Next, the valve cup is crimped to seal the propellant inside the can. Finally, the sunscreen concentrate is pumped into the bag through the valve stem under high pressure, expanding the bag and sealing the active concentrate completely away from the surrounding propellant.</p>

            
